 My Own Feature Ideas (Synthesis)

 These are features I think would be compelling in a combined app, drawing from the patterns above but not directly
 from any single one:

 ### Spatial-Everything Model

 - Infinite canvas as the base — not just a "canvas view" you switch to, but the fundamental data model. Text,
   terminals, databases, spreadsheets, agents — all exist as objects on a spatial canvas. You can arrange them freely,
   group them, resize them. Think Obsidian Canvas × tmux panes × IDE editor groups, unified.
 - Spatially-aware search — search results show where things live in your workspace, not just a list. Click to fly
   there.
 - Spatial undo — moving/resizing objects on canvas is undoable, just like text edits.

 ### Quantum as Universal Edit Scope

 - Everything is a quantum — a quantum is the smallest addressable unit of anything. A character, a line, a function, a
   file, a terminal session, a database row, a chart, a slide. Operations (delete, copy, move, search) work on any
   quantum type. This generalizes vim's text objects to the entire app.
 - Quantum hierarchy — characters ⊂ lines ⊂ paragraphs ⊂ functions ⊂ files ⊂ projects. You can operate at any level. d
   + function deletes a function. d + file deletes a file. d + terminal kills a terminal.
 - Quantum queries — select all quanta matching a predicate: "all files modified today", "all terminal outputs
   containing ERROR", "all notes tagged #urgent".

 ### Unified Command Language

 - One input field for everything — a single command bar (like VS Code's palette + tmux prefix + Excel formula bar)
   that accepts:
     - Text edits (change all foo to bar)
     - Navigation (goto file:src/main.rs:42)
     - App operations (split terminal right, new agent:review)
     - Data queries (SELECT * FROM notes WHERE tag=urgent)
     - AI prompts (refactor this to use async)
     - Spreadsheet formulas (=SUM(A1:A10))
 - Command composition — pipe commands: grep "TODO" | agent:fix | review
 - Command history with context — not just a list, but searchable with "when did I last do X in this
   file/project/context"

 ### Live Data as First-Class Content

 - Streams as documents — tail -f, log files, agent outputs, database subscriptions — all render as live-updating
   documents in the canvas. Scroll through history, pin interesting moments.
 - Reactive bindings — a cell in a spreadsheet can be bound to a terminal output, a database query, an API response.
   When the source changes, the cell updates.
 - Time-travel views — any document can show its state at any point in history. Scrub through time like a video.

 ### Agent-Native Workspace

 - Agents as citizens — AI agents aren't a sidebar or a chat panel. They're objects on the canvas with their own
   terminals, their own files, visible state (working/blocked/done). You can talk to them, watch them work, redirect
   them — all in the same spatial context as your own work.
 - Agent output as data — when an agent produces code, a plan, or a report, it's a quantum. You can edit it, reference
   it, pipe it elsewhere, version it.
 - Swarm views — see all your agents at once in a dashboard: what each is doing, what's blocked, what needs your input.
   One keystroke to focus on any of them.
 - Delegation graph — visualize the hierarchy of delegated tasks: you → firstmate → 3 agents → their sub-tasks. Click
   any node to inspect.

 ### Persistence & Continuity

 - Everything survives — sessions, terminal state, agent work, canvas layout, scrollback. Close the app, open it
   tomorrow, exactly where you left off. Like tmux persistence + VS Code settings sync + Obsidian vault.
 - Session snapshots — save named snapshots of your entire workspace state. Restore, diff, merge snapshots.
 - Cross-device continuity — start on your desktop, continue on your laptop, check from your phone. The workspace IS
   the source of truth, the device is just a viewport.

 ### Fabrication (Sheet/Presentation within the Editor)

 - Spreadsheet mode — any document can be "viewed as" a table. Parse structured text into cells, apply formulas,
   generate charts. Not a separate app — a mode of the same content.
 - Presentation mode — any document (or collection of quanta) can be presented as slides. Auto-generate slides from a
   document's heading structure. Present with speaker notes, transitions.
 - Report generation — compose a report from: live query results + charts + code snippets + prose + images. All on the
   canvas, all live-bound, export to PDF/HTML.

 ### Communication as a Layer

 - Tailscale built in — the app's networking layer. Any device running the app can join a tailnet. Share a workspace, a
   terminal, a document — just by being on the same tailnet.
 - Peer-to-peer document sharing — no server. Two people's workspaces mesh directly. Shared documents sync over the
   tailnet.
 - Remote attach — open the app on any device, attach to a session running elsewhere. Your local instance becomes a
   thin client. (Like herdr's remote, but for the whole app.)
 - Async collaboration — leave a "handoff note" at a specific place in your work. The next person to open that
   workspace sees it, acts on it, leaves their own.

 ### Security & Trust

 - Capability-based permissions — agents, plugins, extensions get explicit capabilities (can read files? can execute?
   can network?). You grant/revoke per-session or per-agent.
 - Audit log — every action (yours or an agent's) is logged with full context. Queryable, filterable, exportable.
 - Sandboxed plugins — WASM or similar. A plugin can't do anything it hasn't been explicitly permitted.
 - Trust levels — per source: your own code (full trust), verified plugins (limited), arbitrary web content (sandboxed,
   read-only).

 ### Search & Discovery

 - Semantic + structural search — search by meaning ("where do we handle auth?") or by structure (function: * login *)
   or by text ("password") — all in one query language.
 - Cross-modal search — search through text, terminal history, database records, agent conversations, file contents,
   canvas objects — one search box, results grouped by type.
 - Graph-aware navigation — "show me everything related to this" as a live graph, not a flat list. Follow edges to
   explore.

 ### Configurability Without Configuration

 - Everything is inspectable and scriptable — there is no "hidden" behavior. Any operation you can do by hand, you can
   script. Any state you can see, you can query.
 - Convention over configuration, but overridable — sensible defaults that just work. Override anything with a simple
   config file. No 2000-line vimrc required.
 - Self-documenting — the app documents itself. Ask "how do I do X?" and get an answer with an example, not a link to a
   47-page manual.

