Asked AI how vim works and how visur works. It appears Vim does not redraw the entire screen for every update which is why it's so fast and you can jump to different pages very quickly. But visur does. See answer below:

 Looked at both sides of the pipe. Answer: yes — full viewport repaint on every keystroke, plus the full
 document over the wire. It's worse than "redraws the screen": nothing anywhere is diffed.

 The path, one keystroke

 1. Every keypress becomes modelWasChanged — KeyWasPressedVerticle.java:31
 (src/main/java/com/ple/visur/KeyWasPressedVerticle.java#L31). No distinction between "cursor moved one
 cell" and "document edited". All keys take the same path.

 2. Full document serialized — ModelWasChangedVerticle.java:20,28
 (src/main/java/com/ple/visur/ModelWasChangedVerticle.java#L19-28):

 ```java
   view.editorContent = emc.getEditorContent();   // whole buffer, every key
   output.put("editorContent", view.editorContent);  // whole buffer on the wire
 ```

 That runs over SockJS/WebSocket. For the Declaration of Independence init buffer (~8 KB) that's 8 KB per
 keystroke. Nothing is a delta.

 3. Full canvas clear — visur.js:72 (src/main/resources/static/visur.js#L72) →
 [clearCanvas():212`](src/main/resources/static/visur.js#L211-213):

 ```js
   ctx.clearRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);   // entire viewport, unconditionally
 ```

 4. Full viewport repaint — drawCanvas():136 (src/main/resources/static/visur.js#L136-160) then loops absX
 from canvasStart to canvasEnd, calling fillText once per character cell (:196
 (src/main/resources/static/visur.js#L196)), plus a fillRect for the cursor. On a 1470×900 window:
 cellWidth ≈ 13, so ~113 × 45 ≈ 5,000 fillText calls per keystroke. There is no dirty-rect tracking, no
 backbuffer, no per-cell diff, no double buffering.

 5. DOM churn on top — visur.js:75-106 (src/main/resources/static/visur.js#L75-106) rewrites innerHTML on
 two status elements every event, sometimes in a chain of appends.

 The asymmetry that matters

 ┌─────────┬───────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┐
 │ Cost    │ Complexity        │ Where                         │
 ├─────────┼───────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
 │ Payload │ O(document)       │ ModelWasChangedVerticle:20,28 │
 ├─────────┼───────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
 │ Repaint │ O(viewport cells) │ drawCanvas, ~5,000 fillText   │
 ├─────────┼───────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
 │ DOM     │ O(status length)  │ visur.js:75-106               │
 └─────────┴───────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘

 Drawing is bounded by the viewport, so it degrades gracefully as files grow. The payload does not — it's
 the whole buffer. That's the load-bearing problem, and it's exactly what filesystem work lands on:

 - Open a 2 MB file → 2 MB shipped per keystroke.
 - Browse a directory with 10,000 entries rendered as listing content → the listing ships whole on every
   cursor move within the listing.

 Also flagging, since I hit it: CanvasWasChangedVerticle.java:20-32
 (src/main/java/com/ple/visur/CanvasWasChangedVerticle.java#L20-32) has a leftover debug loop commented
 "simply a line by line log of editorContent". It loops i over nextLineIndices.size() but reads
 nextLineIndices.get(cy) with cy fixed, so it prints the same line N times to stdout on every canvas
 resize. Dead weight; not a blocker.

 One caveat: my frame-time estimate (~5,000 × 2–5 µs ≈ 10–25 ms/keystroke) is arithmetic, not measurement —
 I haven't run the app.

 What this does to the map

 This is new fog that was invisible until now, and it sits under the browse ticket: designing a directory
 browser on a full-snapshot payload means designing straight into a perf cliff. So it graduates to a
 decision ticket that blocks the browse work —

 "How does the view payload reach the client?" — full snapshot vs. viewport slice vs. delta/patch, and
 whether repaint stays full-viewport or moves to dirty regions. I'd lean: ship a viewport slice + explicit
 change kind (cursor-only vs content), keep full repaint for beta (it's bounded by viewport and cheap to
 reason about), and revisit dirty regions only if measurement says so.
